declare_args() {
  # Per-build customization of what a kernel image carries, as one bundle.
  #
  # This is Ours' counterpart of Fuchsia's `fx set --with`: it lets one build
  # directory say "this image also carries these user programs, and runs that
  # one at boot", so that several sessions can each build and boot their own
  # image side by side instead of sharing one out/bringup.x86. tools/set.py
  # writes it from --with/--autorun; it can also be written into args.gn by
  # hand.
  #
  # It follows Fuchsia's model, in which what boots is the product's and a
  # build only makes more programs available: userboot always starts
  # component_manager there (userboot.next), and `fx set --with` adds packages
  # without touching that. Here the image's boot flow -- userboot, init, the
  # shell -- is likewise fixed by the image and not a field of this bundle.
  #
  # Fields (every one optional):
  #
  #   with
  #     List of plain GN labels -- "//examples/foo", no toolchain suffix --
  #     naming user programs the image carries IN ADDITION to its boot flow.
  #     The image appends the user toolchain itself, because a label resolved
  #     in the kernel toolchain would build a -mcmodel=kernel "user program".
  #     A program named here is built and packaged into the BootFS; it runs
  #     when something that is already running starts it.
  #
  #   autorun
  #     A command init runs once the shell is up: `<BootFS name>[+arg...]`,
  #     with the program's whole BootFS path, `bin/fault_probe+none`,
  #     written into the image's cmdline as `ours.autorun.boot=` -- Ours'
  #     counterpart of Zircon's `zircon.autorun.boot`, which console-launcher
  #     runs after starting the shell. This is how a build asks for its own
  #     program at boot without changing the boot flow: the flow still runs
  #     userboot, init and the shell, and init then starts this command
  #     (system/service/init). The program it names must be in the image --
  #     usually through `with` -- which tools/set.py checks after gn gen.
  #
  # Why one scope and not one argument per field: new knobs land as new fields
  # without growing the flat argument namespace. The cost is GN's semantics for
  # scope arguments: args.gn replaces the default scope wholesale, it does not
  # merge field by field. So the default is empty and each consumer supplies its
  # own default for a missing field (`if (defined(ours.with))`), which is also
  # where those defaults belong -- which programs an image carries by default is
  # a property of that image, not of the build.
  #
  # Consumed by //kernel/products/image/dev only. dev.dbg_1 is a fixed
  # diagnostic image whose program set is its purpose (see its BUILD.gn), and
  # fake stands in for vmours; neither reads this.
  ours = {
  }
}
